{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3332","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-docs-backlog-item-groomed-schema-field-drift.md@sha256:fbe039c152a0307f4e05e96206d092068d86813ccc71ef7b44d2a8b5c47276f7"},"owner_reference":"3332","content":"# Feature Context \u2014 #3332: `docs/backlog-item-groomed-schema.md` field drift and backend-specific framing\n\n**Artifact type**: feature-context (WHAT/WHY discovery)\n**Item**: #3332 \u2014 `fix: docs/backlog-item-groomed-schema.md has field drift vs models.py and backend-specific framing that misleads the groomer agent`\n**Priority**: P1 \u00b7 **Type**: defect \u00b7 **Effort**: XS \u00b7 **RT-ICA**: APPROVED (READY, zero MISSING conditions)\n**Source**: Follow-up from #3329 (dh:backlog dead-skill cleanup)\n\nThis document synthesises the item's completed grooming output (Impact Radius, Fact-Check, RT-ICA, Issue Classification, Design Intent Alignment) into WHAT/WHY discovery framing. Every claim below is already verified at the cited source by the grooming swarm; nothing here is re-derived. **This document deliberately states no implementation approach** \u2014 wording, structure, and edit sequencing belong to the architecture phase.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Problem Space\n\n### 1.1 What the artifact is\n\n`plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-groomed-schema.md` is agent-facing instruction content, not human reference prose. It tells a grooming agent what shape the content it produces must take: frontmatter fields, body sections, and the `## Groomed` subsection inventory that flows through `backlog_groom(groomed_content=...)`.\n\nIt is **live-read content today**. `skills/dh-meta-docs/SKILL.md` indexes every file under the plugin's `docs/` directory via a dynamic `find \u2026 -name '*.md'` glob and instructs the reading agent to \"Read any file above to learn about that topic.\" Because the schema doc lives under that directory, it is already reachable \u2014 no hardcoded path reference is required for an agent to load it. Upstream commit #3330 leaned on exactly this mechanism when it redirected `agents/backlog-item-groomer.md` and `skills/groom-backlog-item/references/groomer-agent.md` away from a broken hardcoded `.claude/docs/...` path and toward `dh:dh-meta-docs` discovery.\n\n### 1.2 What is wrong\n\nThree verified divergences between what the doc asserts and what `backlog_core` actually implements:\n\n| # | Location | Doc asserts | Implementation | Evidence |\n|---|----------|-------------|----------------|----------|\n| 1 | `:15` | Frontmatter title field is `name:` | `BacklogItem` defines `title` / `reference` / `description`; no `name` field exists on `BacklogItem` or `BacklogItemMetadata` | `backlog_core/models.py:1011-1013` (fact-check Claim 1) |\n| 2 | `:5` | Groomed content \"lives in the body of `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/backlog/{priority}-{slug}.md`\" | Storage \"always writes `.yaml` regardless of the original file extension\"; and the per-item-file layout exists only for the GitHub backend's local cache \u2014 sqlite, memory, and beads have no comparable path structure at all | `backlog_core/yaml_io.py:87` (fact-check Claim 2); `AGENTS.md` \u00a7Backend Providers (fact-check Claim 3) |\n| 3 | `:23` | Status vocabulary is `open\\|in-progress\\|done\\|resolved` | `_VALID_STATUSES = {\"open\", \"done\", \"in-progress\", \"needs-grooming\", \"closed\"}` \u2014 the doc omits two canonical values and invents one (`resolved`, preserved only as legacy at `_validate_status`) | `backlog_core/models.py:723` (fact-check Claim 4) |\n\nPoint 2 is the structurally interesting one: it is not merely a stale file extension. The `.md`\u2192`.yaml` slip is a surface error on top of a deeper category error \u2014 the doc presents one backend's physical cache layout as the universal storage mechanism for all four. The statement is wrong in extension for the one backend it half-describes, and wrong in kind for the other three.\n\nPoint 1 has a nuance worth preserving for the architecture phase: `name` is not simply absent from the system. `backlog_core/parsing.py:363` reads `title=str(fm.get(\"name\") or fm.get(\"title\") or \"\")` \u2014 `name` is accepted as a **legacy read-time alias** for backward compatibility with old files, and is never written back. The defect is that the doc presents a legacy input alias as the current output field. Whether the corrected doc should mention that alias at all is an open question (\u00a76, Q1), not a settled fact.\n\n### 1.3 Why it matters (severity rationale)\n\nThe consequence chain is direct and has no mitigating gate. An agent following the doc as written emits a `name:` field the model does not have, describes storage in terms that are false for three of four backends, and selects from a status vocabulary containing an invalid value and missing two valid ones. Nothing downstream catches this at the point of authorship \u2014 it surfaces later, wherever the malformed content lands, and must be corrected by hand.\n\nThis is documentation-only: there is no runtime, interface, or behavioural change to `backlog_core`, and no end-user-facing impact. Severity comes entirely from the doc's position as dispatch-time instruction content for an autonomous agent, not from blast radius in code.\n\n### 1.4 Reproducibility\n\nDeterministic \u2014 a static content-drift defect, reproducible on every read with no environment, timing, or backend-selection condition. Open the file, compare `:15` against `models.py:1011-1013`, `:5` against `yaml_io.py:87` and `AGENTS.md` \u00a7Backend Providers, `:23` against `models.py:723`. All comparisons fail identically every time.\n\n### 1.5 Root cause\n\nClassified `defect`, analysed via 5-whys. The evidence chain establishes that `BacklogItem` never had a `name` field (first definition at `0f3c0fb9b`, 2026-03-18, shows `title`/`description`); that the `.yaml` write path and the pluggable-backend protocol (`a2b56597a`, 2026-03-30) both landed after the doc was written; and that the doc has not been touched since, while `models.py` has changed repeatedly across the same period.\n\n**Root cause**: no documentation-sync gate exists at PR-merge time for this file. Changes to `BacklogItem`, the storage layer, and the backend architecture all proceeded without any requirement \u2014 CI check, review checklist, or otherwise \u2014 to update the schema doc that describes them.\n\nThat root cause is systemic and is **not** what this item fixes. #3332 corrects the accumulated instance. Whether the class deserves its own guardrail is noted in \u00a76 (Q3) as a discovery question, not folded into scope.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Desired Outcome\n\nSuccess is a schema doc an agent can follow literally and produce content that matches `backlog_core`'s real interface \u2014 with no downstream correction required and no backend-conditional truth.\n\nThree observable properties, drawn from the item's Expected Behavior and Acceptance Criteria:\n\n1. **Field accuracy** \u2014 the frontmatter example presents `BacklogItem`'s real fields (`title`, `reference`, `description`, per `models.py:1011-1013`) and does not present `name:` as a current schema element.\n2. **Backend-agnostic framing** \u2014 the storage/\"Location\" description holds true for all four backends (github, sqlite, memory, beads), or explicitly scopes any backend-specific detail *as* backend-specific. The doc's subject becomes the content passed through `backlog_groom(groomed_content=...)` \u2014 frontmatter shape, body sections, `## Groomed` subsections \u2014 which is identical regardless of which backend is configured.\n3. **Status vocabulary accuracy** \u2014 the example matches `_VALID_STATUSES` (`open`, `done`, `in-progress`, `needs-grooming`, `closed`) with no non-canonical values.\n\nPlus one scope constraint the item states as an acceptance criterion in its own right: **no other file is modified**. #3332's diff is `docs/backlog-item-groomed-schema.md` and nothing else.\n\n### What \"done\" is not\n\n- Not a rewrite of the doc's other content. The `## Groomed` section inventory (`:64-81`) was already reconciled against `section_registry.py` by #2979's `KNOWN_ENTITIES.md` pass with no drift found \u2014 explicitly out of scope.\n- Not a fix to the sibling `templates/backlog-item.md`, which carries the same `name`/`title` drift (\u00a74).\n- Not a documentation-sync guardrail (\u00a76, Q3).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Stakeholders\n\n| Stakeholder | Relationship | What changes for them |\n|---|---|---|\n| **`dh:backlog-item-groomer` agent** | Primary consumer. Reads this doc as its Output Format schema reference, reached via `dh:dh-meta-docs` since #3330. | Stops being instructed to emit a nonexistent field and an invalid status value. |\n| **`skills/groom-backlog-item` dispatch prompts** (`references/groomer-agent.md`) | Second live consumer, same discovery route since #3330. | Same. |\n| **Any future agent loading `docs/*.md`** | The `dh-meta-docs` glob is dynamic \u2014 every current and future doc-reading agent reaches this file without being registered anywhere. | The blast radius of an inaccurate doc here is open-ended by design; correcting it closes that exposure. |\n| **`docs/workflow-layers/KNOWN_ENTITIES.md`** | Cites this doc as a provenance cross-check source at `L314`/`L357`, **by path and line range** (`L64\u201381`), not by field content. | Not affected by the field drift itself, but its cited line range may shift if the edit changes line counts above `:64`. Worth a post-edit verification, per Impact Radius. |\n| **Backlog contributors / reviewers** | Indirect. | Fewer malformed-frontmatter corrections to catch in review. |\n\nNon-stakeholders, stated explicitly: end users (no runtime surface), `backlog_core` callers (no interface change), CI (no workflow or config file references this doc \u2014 grepped repo-wide across `.github` and all `*.yml`/`*.yaml`, zero hits).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Risks and Constraints\n\n### R1 \u2014 Worktree is behind `origin/main` (highest-value pre-work risk)\n\nThe grooming pass established that this worktree's `main` trails `origin/main`, and that the missing commits include #3330, which already landed consumer-side fixes overlapping this item's stated starting points:\n\n- Both named \"broken path\" consumers (`agents/backlog-item-groomer.md`, `skills/groom-backlog-item/references/groomer-agent.md`) are **already fixed** upstream.\n- `skills/backlog-tools-administrator/references/domain-registry.md:72`, cited in the original problem statement as a third broken-path site, **no longer exists** \u2014 #3330 deleted the whole skill.\n- The target file itself is **untouched** by #3330 (`git show 20c84f3b5 -- plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-groomed-schema.md` returns an empty diff), so the core fix remains valid and necessary.\n\nNet: the real remaining work is *smaller* than the item's original framing implied, and planning against this worktree's state rather than `origin/main` risks producing a diff that duplicates or conflicts with already-merged work. Verify against `origin/main` before planning. This is a pre-implementation check, not in-scope work.\n\n### R2 \u2014 Scope creep toward `templates/backlog-item.md`\n\n`plugins/development-harness/templates/backlog-item.md` (the post-#3330 replacement for the deleted `skills/backlog/templates/item.md`) still uses `name: ITEM TITLE HERE` \u2014 the identical drift class. The `name`/`title` divergence has *moved* upstream, not disappeared.\n\nRead broadly (\"make the docs match the model\"), fixing it belongs here. Read as written, the item's mandate names one file, and its own acceptance criteria forbid touching others. Impact Radius and RT-ICA both recommend a **separate follow-up item** over silently widening this diff. Discovery records the tension; it does not need resolving before architecture, but it should be a deliberate call rather than a drift.\n\n### R3 \u2014 \"Backend-agnostic\" is an outcome, not yet a specified shape\n\nAC #4 permits two distinct outcomes: a description that is universally true, *or* one that keeps backend-specific detail but labels it as such. Both satisfy the criterion. Which one serves the reading agent better is an architecture-phase decision (\u00a76, Q2). The risk is settling it implicitly during editing rather than deliberately.\n\n### R4 \u2014 Line-number churn in a doc cited by line range\n\n`KNOWN_ENTITIES.md` cites `docs/backlog-item-groomed-schema.md (L64\u201381)`. Edits at `:5`, `:15`, and `:23` that change line counts will shift that range. Low severity, easily verified, but easily forgotten.\n\n### C1 \u2014 No test surface\n\nThis is prose. There is no test suite impact, no structured check, and nothing that fails if the fix regresses. Verification is by reading the file against the cited sources. The acceptance criteria are written to be checkable by inspection precisely because nothing else can check them.\n\n### C2 \u2014 Documentation-only, no interface change\n\nNothing in `backlog_core` changes. No deprecation, no migration, no compatibility surface. This constrains what the fix may do as much as it lowers its risk: correcting the doc must not imply an interface change that has not happened.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Design Intent Alignment\n\nVerdict: **MISSION_ALIGNED**. The alignment analysis found the fix does more than remove errors \u2014 it enforces a principle the harness states explicitly.\n\n- **Logical vs. physical boundary** \u2014 `AGENTS.md` \u00a7State Management: \"Physical paths, cache records, provider IDs, and wire formats are backend internals.\" The `:5` \"Location\" framing is precisely a physical path leaking into agent-facing instruction. Removing it is the design principle being applied, not merely a correction.\n- **Agent-facing workflow integrity** \u2014 `PURPOSE.md` \u00a7Purpose: the plugin is \"primarily an agent-facing workflow system expressed in Markdown.\" When Markdown *is* the interface, an inaccurate doc is a broken interface, not a cosmetic flaw. This is the substantive justification for P1 on a documentation-only item.\n- **Consumer documentation boundary** \u2014 `PURPOSE.md` \u00a7Documentation Frames: consumer documentation must stand alone for ordinary use. The agent is the consumer here.\n- **Multi-backend abstraction** \u2014 the schema doc should express backend-agnostic structure, not embed one backend's layout assumptions.\n\nNo divergence found. Scope is a documentation-accuracy fix that preserves all existing grooming behaviour.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Open Questions\n\nCarried into architecture. None block planning \u2014 RT-ICA is APPROVED with zero MISSING conditions.\n\n**Q1 \u2014 Should the corrected doc mention `name` as a legacy read alias, or omit it entirely?**\n`parsing.py:363` accepts `name` on read for backward compatibility with old files but never writes it. Mentioning it is accurate and helps an agent reading an old file; omitting it is cleaner and removes any chance of an agent re-emitting it. The acceptance criterion only forbids presenting `name` \"as a documented/current schema element\" \u2014 both readings satisfy it. A judgement call about what serves the reading agent, not a factual gap.\n\n**Q2 \u2014 Universal statement, or explicitly-scoped backend-specific detail? (see R3)**\nAC #4 allows either. Related: should the doc describe storage *at all*, given that the content schema is what the agent needs and storage is a backend internal the harness's own design principle says agents should not address?\n\n**Q3 \u2014 Does the root cause warrant its own item?**\nThe 5-whys terminates at \"no documentation-sync gate exists at PR-merge time.\" #3332 fixes one instance; `templates/backlog-item.md` (R2) is a second live instance of the same class in the same repo. Two known instances is a signal. Whether that becomes a guardrail item is a product decision outside this item's scope \u2014 recorded here so it is not lost with the fix.\n\n**Q4 \u2014 Are there further unnamed drift points in the same file?**\nThe item names three. Two further candidates were observed during this discovery pass and are flagged in Concerns below rather than folded into scope; whether they belong in #3332's diff or a follow-up is an architecture-phase call. Both are cheap to verify.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Evidence Index\n\nAll primary sources cited by the grooming swarm, for the architecture phase to work from directly:\n\n| Source | Establishes |\n|---|---|\n| `backlog_core/models.py:1011-1013` | Canonical `BacklogItem` fields: `title`, `reference`, `description` |\n| `backlog_core/models.py:723` | `_VALID_STATUSES = {\"open\", \"done\", \"in-progress\", \"needs-grooming\", \"closed\"}` |\n| `backlog_core/models.py:882-896` | `_validate_status` \u2014 `resolved` preserved as legacy, not canonical |\n| `backlog_core/models.py:760-796` | `BacklogItemMetadata` \u2014 the typed metadata sub-model persisted to frontmatter |\n| `backlog_core/yaml_io.py:87` | `save_item()` \u2014 \"Always writes `.yaml` format regardless of the original file extension\" |\n| `backlog_core/parsing.py:363`, `:1368` | `name` accepted as legacy read-time alias only, never written back |\n| `backlog_core/operations.py` (`_write_groomed_to_item`, `_write_groomed_to_reference`, `_apply_groomed_update`, `_handle_update_groomed`) | The write path that persists `## Groomed` content per this schema |\n| `backlog_core/backends/{github,sqlite,memory,beads}_backend.py` | Four backends; only GitHub's local cache uses the per-item-file layout |\n| `AGENTS.md` \u00a7Backend Providers | \"Beads, SQLite, and Memory use native storage directly and never read/write backlog YAML\" |\n| `AGENTS.md` \u00a7State Management | Logical-vs-physical boundary principle |\n| `PURPOSE.md` \u00a7Purpose, \u00a7Documentation Frames | Agent-facing Markdown as interface; consumer-documentation boundary |\n| `skills/dh-meta-docs/SKILL.md` | Dynamic `docs/*.md` glob \u2014 the discovery mechanism making this doc live |\n| `docs/workflow-layers/KNOWN_ENTITIES.md:314`, `:357` | Path+line-range citation of this doc (see R4) |\n| commit `20c84f3b5` (#3330) | Consumer-side path fixes already landed; target file untouched |\n| commit `0f3c0fb9b` (2026-03-18) | First `BacklogItem` definition \u2014 `title`/`description`, never `name` |\n| commit `a2b56597a` (2026-03-30) | Pluggable `BacklogBackend` protocol \u2014 when \"Location\" framing became backend-specific |\n\n---\n\n## Concerns\n\nObservations from this discovery pass. Not folded into #3332's scope; recorded for the architecture phase to accept or defer.\n\n**C1 \u2014 `name` appears twice in the target file, but only one occurrence is covered by the acceptance criteria.**\n`:15` is the frontmatter example, named in the item. `:46` is a prose rule \u2014 \"Do **not** duplicate `name`, `description`, `source`, `added`, `priority`, `type`, `issue` in the body\" \u2014 which also treats `name` as a current field. AC #1 speaks only to \"the frontmatter example.\" Fixing `:15` while leaving `:46` would leave the doc self-inconsistent and still naming a nonexistent field. Cheap to include; needs to be a deliberate decision rather than an oversight.\n\n**C2 \u2014 The doc's own \"No line numbers\" rule (`:58`) contradicts how this very item was groomed.**\nThe rule reads: \"Reference files and function names only. Line numbers go stale as code changes. Use `path/to/file.py \u2014 FunctionName()` not `path/to/file.py:42`.\" #3332's own groomed sections are built almost entirely on line-number citations (`models.py:1011-1013`, `models.py:723`, `yaml_io.py:87`), and the fact-checker's verdicts depend on them. The rule and the practice disagree; the practice appears to be the better one for verifiable evidence, and the rule may be the thing that is wrong. This is not #3332's problem to solve, but it is the same failure mode \u2014 instruction content that no longer matches reality \u2014 one section below the drift being fixed. Worth a follow-up.\n\n**C3 \u2014 `:80` states a condition an agent cannot evaluate.**\nThe `Issue Classification` row's Required column reads \"When groomed after Issue #314.\" An agent reading the schema has no way to determine whether the item in front of it postdates #314. Unversioned temporal conditions in agent-facing instruction are unactionable. Flagged, not in scope.\n\n**C4 \u2014 A fourth drift candidate at `:21`, unverified against the item's scope.**\nThe doc gives `priority: P0|P1|P2|Ideas`, while `models.py:721` defines `_VALID_PRIORITIES = {\"P0\", \"P1\", \"P2\", \"Ideas\", \"completed\"}` \u2014 `completed` is missing from the doc. Same class as the status-vocabulary defect the item *does* name (`:23`), one line apart, and equally cheap to verify. It is not in the acceptance criteria. Either it belongs in this diff or the item's framing of \"the status vocabulary\" was narrower than the underlying problem. (`_VALID_TYPES` at `:22` was checked and **does** match \u2014 `Feature|Bug|Refactor|Docs|Chore` is correct.)\n\n**C5 \u2014 Broken relative link at `:11`.**\nThe doc links `[research/README.md](../../research/README.md)`. Resolved from the doc's own location (`plugins/development-harness/docs/`), `../../` lands at `plugins/`, so the target is `plugins/research/README.md` \u2014 which does not exist. The intended target is almost certainly the repository-root `research/README.md` (which does exist), meaning the link needs one more `../`. The `metadata:` block framing the link supports is itself **correct** \u2014 `BacklogItem.metadata: BacklogItemMetadata` is real (`models.py:1018`), so the nesting shown in the example is accurate and is *not* drift. Only the link path is broken.\n\n**C6 \u2014 The one file that would catch this class of defect is the one nobody reads on write.**\n`KNOWN_ENTITIES.md` already performs provenance cross-checks against this doc (#2979 reconciled the `## Groomed` section table against `section_registry.py` and found no drift). That mechanism worked \u2014 for the sections it covered. The frontmatter fields and status vocabulary were not covered, and those are exactly where the drift accumulated. This suggests the root cause (\u00a71.5) is narrower and more tractable than \"no doc-sync gate exists\": a partial cross-check ledger exists and simply does not extend to frontmatter. Relevant to Q3 if a guardrail item is ever raised.\n\n## Post-Implementation Annotations\n\nAdded by context-refinement agent on 2026-08-31\n\n### Design Refinements\n\n1. Single-file AC #6 is unsatisfiable under the repo's auto-sync-manifests pre-commit hook: `plugins/development-harness/.pre-commit-config.yaml:160-166` (`files: ^plugins/`, `pass_filenames: false`) unconditionally bumps `.claude-plugin/plugin.json`, `.codex-plugin/plugin.json`, and `.cursor-plugin/plugin.json` on every commit touching `plugins/`. This is a repo-wide side effect, not something the implementing task authored or could avoid without bypassing a repository-mandated hook.\n   - Original: \"no other file is modified\" (this document's own \u00a72 restatement of item AC #6).\n   - Actual: the landed commit modifies 4 files \u2014 the target doc plus the three manifests.\n   - Recorded in: P74e3c201/T2 (GAPS_FOUND verdict), independently reproduced in quality-gate code-review artifact `code-review-T1-qg-docs-backlog-item-groomed-schema-field-drift`.\n   - Note: No `Intent Source` reference is present on this artifact (pre-policy artifact), so this is classified design-refinement per the context-refinement policy default rather than intent-divergence.\n\n2. The architect spec's Edit 2 replacement text for \u00a71.1's own cited phrase \u2014 \"passed through `backlog_groom(groomed_content=...)`\" \u2014 is itself inaccurate: `backlog_groom()` has no `groomed_content` parameter (`backlog_core/server.py:2550-2616` defines `selector`, `section`, `content`, `entry_id`, `replace_section`, `reason`, `append`, `sections`, `mark_groomed`). This document's own \u00a71.1 opening paragraph repeats the same inaccurate call shape, inherited from a stale characterization of `backlog_groom()` predating the discovery pass.\n   - Actual: correct framing is `backlog_groom(selector=..., section=..., content=...)`.\n   - Recorded in: `doc-drift-audit-backlog-item-groomed-schema` artifact (2026-08-31).\n   - Action needed: A follow-up fix to `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-groomed-schema.md` line 5 is required; not blocking for #3332's own nine ACs but is an active drift as of this annotation.\n"}